## What a `type: tag` instance is

A canonical first-class tag entity — the substrate's reflective representation of a classification keyword.  Tags exist as their own memorial entities (not just strings on other memorials), allowing them to carry definition, scope-notes, hierarchy (broader/narrower), alternate labels, and provenance.  The first-class-tag pattern transforms tagging from informal annotation into structured controlled-vocabulary that's introspectable, auditable, and consolidatable.

## When to use a canonical tag (vs free-text)

The corpus supports both free-text tagging AND canonical tags — the DCAT two-tier pattern.  Use a canonical `:mm/Tag` when:

- The tag is reused across multiple memorials (≥3 occurrences)
- The tag carries meaning beyond its surface form (definition, scope, hierarchy)
- The tag participates in audit invariants (e.g., closure-consistency)
- The tag could be confused with similar-shaped tags (synonyms, plurals, abbreviations) — promote to canonical with alt-labels

Stay informal (free-text on `:mm.memory/tags`) when:
- The tag is one-off ("2026-01-04-phase-3" style)
- The tag is intentionally lossy (a temporary marker)
- The tag's meaning is fully captured by its surface form

